hool yes business  school for the same reason as most of  you  to take outsized bets on my career  i want to co-found and invest in amazing  unicorns  build the best climate tech industry in  the whole world  thanks stanford  maybe publish a book and really go big  but as i began achieving these goals  and i talked to over a hundred companies  in the climate tech space in the past  year  i couldn't help but notice an absence  you see  we are obsessed  with becoming  identifying  the best  players on the field and i think that's  great  but what will make the night and day  difference  for our future  is in fact  influencing and electing  the referees who are going to change the  much needed rules of the game  and i'm telling you those rules have to  be changed in the fight against climate  in order to make the difference between  the future i described and the one that  you want your children to see  i want to say that i too  am a player on the field i've been a  player three times starting at the gsb  and we haven't won a match yet the first  was when i met my classmate stella  on zoom before starting business school  we were stoked by the idea of creating  the platform that will transform carbon  emission tracking for the food supply  space  now we somehow fold some phds to join us  drank way too much coffee and we were  pitching to some of the best vcs on  silicon valley uh sand hill road within  three months  wow i was shocked but so excited  and  said 1vc  love your passion love your idea  but  this is rooted far too heavily in the  policy space political incentives  standard setting  that's not where i put my money i pass  sorry  me in my head  you  pass are you serious  will climate change not affect you too  if you don't fund this solution  who will  this wake up call was extremely humbling  but it taught me two important things  one  of course the investor had a right to  10x returns  that's their job  but more importantly  entrepreneurship and the private 